plan.text:
{"t":"scope","verdict":"in","reason":"Solving linear equations is core school algebra."}
{"t":"warmup","id":"w1","prompt":"3x + 5 = 20. Guess: what is the first sensible move to find x?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Subtract 5 from both sides","correct":true},{"id":"b","text":"Divide only the 3x by 3","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Add 5 to both sides","correct":false}]}
{"t":"objective","text":"Understand how to solve 3x + 5 = 20 and why both sides must change together."}
{"t":"segment","n":1,"title":"An equation is a balance","goal":"See an equation as two equal sides, so any move must keep them equal.","representation":"analogy","misconception":"Changing only one side, as if the equals sign just means 'the answer comes next'","check":"choice"}
{"t":"segment","n":2,"title":"Undoing the plus 5","goal":"Subtract 5 from both sides to isolate the 3x term.","representation":"worked","misconception":"Adding 5 to undo +5, or subtracting it from only one side","check":"expr"}
{"t":"segment","n":3,"title":"Undoing the times 3","goal":"Divide both sides by 3 to get x, then finish the solution.","representation":"numberline","misconception":"Dividing only the 3x by 3 and leaving 15 alone, giving x = 15","check":"number"}
{"t":"segment","n":4,"title":"Check it on a graph","goal":"See the solution as where y = 3x + 5 meets y = 20.","representation":"graph","misconception":"Thinking a solution is just a number, not where two graphs cross","check":"tap"}

segment:1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Think of a balance scale with two pans that sit level."}
{"t":"page","title":"An equation is a balance"}
{"t":"write","id":"s1a","text":"Left pan: 3x + 5   |   Right pan: 20","role":"line"}
{"t":"write","id":"s1b","text":"The scale is level, so both pans weigh the same.","role":"note"}
{"t":"say","text":"That level scale is exactly what the equals sign says."}
{"t":"math","id":"s1e","latex":"3x + 5 = 20","expr":"3*x + 5 = 20"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"underline","target":"s1e","note":"left side equals right side"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now take 5 off the left pan only, and see what happens to the scale."}
{"t":"write","id":"s1w","text":"Left: 3x   |   Right: 20 (untouched)","role":"line"}
{"t":"write","id":"s1t","text":"The scale tips! 3x is now lighter than 20.","role":"note"}
{"t":"say","text":"Changing one side only breaks the equality, so the equals sign is no longer true."}
{"t":"math","id":"s1x","latex":"3x = 20 \\quad \\text{(not the same equation)}","expr":"3*x = 20"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s1x","note":"only one side changed"}
{"t":"say","text":"The fix is to take 5 off the right pan too, and the scale stays level."}
{"t":"math","id":"s1ok","latex":"3x + 5 - 5 = 20 - 5","expr":"3*x + 5 - 5 = 20 - 5"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"s1ok","note":"same move on both sides"}
{"t":"say","text":"So the equals sign is not 'the answer comes next'; it means both sides stay equal."}
{"t":"ask","id":"s1q1","text":"Why does it have to be the same on both sides?"}
{"t":"ask","id":"s1q2","text":"Does this work for multiplying and dividing too?"}
{"t":"check","id":"s1c","kind":"choice","prompt":"You start with 3x + 5 = 20. Which move keeps the equation true?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Subtract 5 from both sides","correct":true},{"id":"b","text":"Subtract 5 from the left side only: 3x = 20","correct":false,"misconception":"Changed just one side, treating the equals sign as 'the answer comes next'"},{"id":"c","text":"Subtract 5 from the left and add 5 to the right","correct":false,"misconception":"Did a different thing to each side, thinking the moves only need to look balanced"},{"id":"d","text":"Subtract 20 from the left side only","correct":false,"misconception":"Used a number from the right side to change just the left, ignoring that both pans must change together"}],"explain":"Doing the same thing to both sides keeps the scale level, so the equation stays true.","hint":"Whatever you do to one pan, what must you do to the other?"}

segment:2.text:
{"t":"page","title":"Undoing the plus 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"Here is our equation again: three x, plus 5, equals 20."}
{"t":"math","id":"s2e","latex":"3x + 5 = 20","expr":"3*x + 5 = 20"}
{"t":"say","text":"The 5 is being added to 3x, so we undo it by subtracting 5."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s2e","note":"the +5 is stuck to 3x"}
{"t":"say","text":"Subtract 5 from both sides, so the balance stays level."}
{"t":"step","id":"s2s","latex":"3x + 5 - 5 = 20 - 5","expr":"3*x + 5 - 5 = 20 - 5","why":"subtract 5 from both sides","from":"s2e"}
{"t":"say","text":"On the left the plus 5 and minus 5 cancel, and on the right 20 minus 5 is 15."}
{"t":"step","id":"s2r","latex":"3x = 15","expr":"3*x = 15","why":"simplify both sides","from":"s2s"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"s2r","note":"3x is now alone"}
{"t":"say","text":"A tempting move is adding 5 instead, because it feels like the opposite of the plus."}
{"t":"math","id":"s2w","latex":"3x + 5 + 5 = 20 + 5 \\Rightarrow 3x + 10 = 25","expr":"3*x + 5 + 5 = 20 + 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"That makes the left side bigger, with 10 added to 3x, so nothing has been undone."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"underline","target":"s2w","note":"+10, not gone"}
{"t":"say","text":"Another slip is subtracting 5 from the left only, which tips the balance."}
{"t":"math","id":"s2w2","latex":"3x = 20 \\text{ (wrong: right side untouched)}"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now you try: undo the plus 7 in a new equation."}
{"t":"ask","id":"s2q1","text":"How do I know which way to undo it?"}
{"t":"ask","id":"s2q2","text":"Does it matter if I do the right side first?"}
{"t":"check","id":"s2c","kind":"expr","prompt":"Start with 2x + 7 = 19. Subtract 7 from both sides and write the result as an equation's left side: what is 2x + 7 − 7 on the left, and 19 − 7 on the right? Give the right side.","answer":"12","variable":"x","anticipated":[{"value":"26","misconception":"Added 7 instead of subtracting it, thinking the opposite of plus is plus"},{"value":"19","misconception":"Subtracted 7 from the left side only and left the right side unchanged"}],"explain":"Subtracting 7 from both sides leaves 2x = 12.","hint":"Whatever you do to the left, do the same to the 19."}

segment:3.text:
{"t":"say","text":"After subtracting 5 from both sides, we are left with three x equals fifteen."}
{"t":"math","id":"s3e","latex":"3x = 15","expr":"3*x = 15"}
{"t":"say","text":"Here 3x means three lots of x, so we want to undo that multiplying."}
{"t":"write","id":"s3w","text":"3x means 3 × x","role":"note"}
{"t":"say","text":"A tempting move is to divide only the 3x by 3 and leave the 15 alone."}
{"t":"math","id":"s3bad","latex":"x = 15","expr":"x = 15"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s3bad","note":"15 is not changed, so the sides no longer balance"}
{"t":"say","text":"Test it: three times 15 is 45, not 15, so that move broke the balance."}
{"t":"math","id":"s3t","latex":"3 \\times 15 = 45 \\neq 15","expr":"3*15 = 45"}
{"t":"say","text":"The fix is to divide both whole sides by 3, just as we did with the 5."}
{"t":"step","id":"s3s","latex":"x = \\frac{15}{3}","expr":"x = 15/3","why":"divide both sides by 3","from":"s3e"}
{"t":"say","text":"Fifteen split into three equal parts gives five, so mark it on a number line."}
{"t":"numberline","id":"s3n","range":[0,10],"marks":[{"at":5,"label":"x = 5"},{"at":0,"label":"0"},{"at":10,"label":"10"}]}
{"t":"say","text":"Now try a similar one yourself, with a different number in front of x."}
{"t":"ask","id":"s3q1","text":"Why divide both sides, not just one?"}
{"t":"ask","id":"s3q2","text":"How can I check my answer is right?"}
{"t":"check","id":"s3c","kind":"number","prompt":"Solve 4x + 3 = 27. What is x?","answer":"6","anticipated":[{"value":"24","misconception":"Subtracted 3 but then did not divide the 24 by 4, leaving x = 24"},{"value":"7.5","misconception":"Divided 30 by 4 after adding 3 instead of subtracting it"}],"explain":"Subtract 3 to get 4x = 24, then divide both sides by 4 to get x = 6.","hint":"First subtract 3 from both sides, then divide the whole other side by 4."}

segment:4.text:
{"t":"say","text":"We found x equals 5, so now let's see that answer on a graph."}
{"t":"math","id":"s4e","latex":"3x + 5 = 20","expr":"3*x + 5 = 20"}
{"t":"say","text":"Treat each side as its own graph: the left side is a slanted line, the right side is flat."}
{"t":"math","id":"s4l","latex":"y = 3x + 5","expr":"y = 3*x + 5"}
{"t":"math","id":"s4r","latex":"y = 20","expr":"y = 20"}
{"t":"say","text":"Here are both lines drawn together."}
{"t":"plot","id":"s4p","x":[-1,8],"y":[-2,25],"fns":[{"id":"s4f1","expr":"3*x + 5","label":"y = 3x + 5"},{"id":"s4f2","expr":"20","label":"y = 20"}]}
{"t":"say","text":"The two sides are equal exactly where the lines cross, and that spot sits above x equals 5."}
{"t":"guide","id":"s4g","plot":"s4p","x":5,"label":"x = 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"Check by putting 5 into the slanted line: it lands right on 20."}
{"t":"math","id":"s4k","latex":"3(5) + 5 = 20","expr":"3*5 + 5 = 20"}
{"t":"say","text":"It's tempting to read the answer as 20, but 20 is a height, not where we are along x."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"s4r","note":"a height, not the answer"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"arrow","target":"s4g","note":"the answer lives along the x-axis"}
{"t":"say","text":"Here are three marked spots; the crossing point is the one where both sides are equal."}
{"t":"point","id":"s4pa","plot":"s4p","at":[0,5],"on":"s4f1"}
{"t":"point","id":"s4pb","plot":"s4p","at":[5,20],"on":"s4f1"}
{"t":"point","id":"s4pc","plot":"s4p","at":[0,20],"on":"s4f2"}
{"t":"ask","id":"s4q1","text":"Why does crossing mean the sides are equal?"}
{"t":"ask","id":"s4q2","text":"Could the lines never cross?"}
{"t":"check","id":"s4c","kind":"tap","prompt":"Tap the point that shows the solution of 3x + 5 = 20.","figure":"s4p","answer":"s4pb","explain":"At the crossing, both sides equal 20, and it sits above x = 5.","hint":"Find where the slanted line and the flat line meet."}

reteach:s1c:b.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's test your move with a number we know works: x equals 5 makes the equation true."}
{"t":"math","id":"r1a","latex":"3(5) + 5 = 20","expr":"3*5 + 5 = 20"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"r1a","note":"x = 5 is a true solution"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now apply your move to the left side only, and put x equals 5 into the new line."}
{"t":"write","id":"r1b","text":"3x = 20 → 3(5) = 15, but the right side says 20","role":"line"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"r1b","note":"15 ≠ 20, the line broke"}
{"t":"say","text":"On a graph, the original line meets the height 20 at x equals 5, but your new line meets it further right."}
{"t":"plot","id":"r1p","x":[0,8],"y":[0,30],"fns":[{"id":"r1f1","expr":"3*x + 5","label":"y = 3x + 5"},{"id":"r1f2","expr":"3*x","label":"y = 3x"},{"id":"r1f3","expr":"20","label":"y = 20"}]}
{"t":"point","id":"r1v","plot":"r1p","at":[5,20],"label":"true solution (5, 20)","on":"r1f1"}
{"t":"say","text":"So that move changed the answer, which means the equation no longer says the same thing; have another go at the checkpoint."}

reteach:s1c:c.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's test your idea on a smaller equation first: x plus 2 equals 5."}
{"t":"math","id":"r1a","latex":"x + 2 = 5","expr":"x + 2 = 5"}
{"t":"write","id":"r1b","text":"We can see x = 3 works: 3 + 2 = 5","role":"note"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now try your move: take 2 from the left, but add 2 to the right."}
{"t":"math","id":"r1c","latex":"x + 2 - 2 = 5 + 2","expr":"x + 2 - 2 = 5 + 2"}
{"t":"write","id":"r1d","text":"That gives x = 7. But 7 + 2 = 9, not 5.","role":"line"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"r1d","note":"the equation broke"}
{"t":"say","text":"Different moves on each side changed the equation, so we lost the true answer."}
{"t":"say","text":"The same thing happens with 3x plus 5: your moves would give 3x equals 25."}
{"t":"write","id":"r1e","text":"3x = 25, so x = 25 ÷ 3","role":"line"}
{"t":"write","id":"r1f","text":"Check: 3 × 25/3 + 5 = 30, not 20","role":"line"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"r1f","note":"does not fit the start"}
{"t":"say","text":"A move is safe only if the new equation still has the same answer as the old one."}
{"t":"say","text":"With that test in mind, have another go at the checkpoint."}

reteach:s1c:d.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's test your move with a number, using x equals 5, which we know makes the equation true."}
{"t":"page","title":"Test the move with a number"}
{"t":"math","id":"r1a","latex":"3(5) + 5 = 20","expr":"3*5 + 5 = 20"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now take 20 away from the left side only, and leave the right side alone."}
{"t":"math","id":"r1b","latex":"3(5) + 5 - 20 = 0","expr":"3*5 + 5 - 20 = 0"}
{"t":"write","id":"r1c","text":"Left is now 0, but the right is still 20. 0 ≠ 20","role":"line"}
{"t":"say","text":"The same x that made it true now makes it false, so that move broke the equation."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"r1c","note":"no longer equal"}
{"t":"say","text":"Compare a move that does the same thing to both sides, here taking away 2."}
{"t":"math","id":"r1d","latex":"3(5) + 5 - 2 = 20 - 2","expr":"3*5 + 5 - 2 = 20 - 2"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"r1d","note":"18 = 18, still true"}
{"t":"say","text":"Keep that test in mind, then try the checkpoint again."}

reteach:s2c:a0.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's test the idea that undoing a plus means adding again, using a table of starting numbers."}
{"t":"table","id":"r2t","head":["Start","After +7","Add 7 again","Take 7 away"],"rows":[["1","8","15","1"],["2","9","16","2"],["4","11","18","4"]]}
{"t":"say","text":"Adding 7 a second time lands further away from where we started, every single row."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"r2t","note":"adding again never returns to the start"}
{"t":"say","text":"Taking 7 away is the only move that walks back to the start, so that is what undoing a plus means."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"underline","target":"r2t","note":"undo = the opposite move"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now look at the left side of the checkpoint again and try it, using the opposite move on both sides."}

reteach:s2c:a1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's test your move on a smaller balance, where we can check every answer."}
{"t":"page","title":"Testing one-sided moves"}
{"t":"math","id":"r2e","latex":"x + 3 = 8","expr":"x + 3 = 8"}
{"t":"say","text":"Try some x values: the sides only match at one of them."}
{"t":"table","id":"r2t","head":["x","left: x + 3","right: 8"],"rows":[["4","7","8"],["5","8","8"],["6","9","8"]]}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"r2t","note":"sides match at one x only"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now take 3 off the left only, leaving the right alone, so the equation claims x equals 8."}
{"t":"math","id":"r2b","latex":"8 + 3 = 11 \\neq 8","expr":"8 + 3 = 11"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"r2b","note":"x = 8 breaks the original"}
{"t":"say","text":"Changing one side moved the answer, so the right side must lose the same amount; try the checkpoint again."}

reteach:s2c:other.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's test a table: take x equals 3, where both sides of 2x + 7 = 13 match."}
{"t":"table","id":"r2t","head":["Side","Before","After −7 on left only"],"rows":[["Left: 2x + 7","13","6"],["Right: 13","13","13"]]}
{"t":"say","text":"Take 7 off just one side and the balance tips: 6 against 13, no longer equal."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"r2t","note":"6 ≠ 13: the balance broke"}
{"t":"say","text":"So whatever happens to the left must also happen to the right, in the same amount."}
{"t":"say","text":"Now try the checkpoint again, giving the right side after taking 7 from both."}

reteach:s3c:a0.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's test your idea: put x = 24 back into the original equation and see if it balances."}
{"t":"math","id":"r3a","latex":"4x + 3 = 27","expr":"4*x + 3 = 27"}
{"t":"math","id":"r3b","latex":"4(24) + 3 = 99","expr":"4*24 + 3 = 99"}
{"t":"say","text":"The left side comes out far bigger than 27, so 24 can't be x."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"r3b","note":"99 is not 27"}
{"t":"say","text":"Here's what went wrong: after taking away 3, the 24 is what the whole 4x equals."}
{"t":"step","id":"r3c","latex":"4x = 24","expr":"4*x = 24","why":"subtract 3 from both sides","from":"r3a"}
{"t":"say","text":"So 24 is four copies of x added together, and x is just one of those copies."}
{"t":"write","id":"r3d","text":"4 × x = 24 means four equal parts make 24","role":"note"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"underline","target":"r3c","note":"24 is the total, not x"}
{"t":"say","text":"Work out what one copy must be, then try the checkpoint again."}

reteach:s3c:a1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"You added 3 to get 30, then divided by 4; let's test that idea by putting your answer back in."}
{"t":"math","id":"r3eq","latex":"4x + 3 = 27","expr":"4*x + 3 = 27"}
{"t":"math","id":"r3test","latex":"4 \\times 7.5 + 3 = 33","expr":"4*7.5 + 3 = 33"}
{"t":"say","text":"We needed 27 but got 33, so that move can't be right."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"r3test","note":"too big: not 27"}
{"t":"say","text":"The equation says 3 was added to 4x, so undoing it means taking 3 away, not adding more."}
{"t":"step","id":"r3s","latex":"4x + 3 - 3 = 27 - 3","expr":"4*x + 3 - 3 = 27 - 3","why":"undo the + 3 with the opposite, on both sides","from":"r3eq"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"underline","target":"r3s","note":"opposite of adding is subtracting"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now try the checkpoint again: subtract first, then undo the times 4, and test your answer by putting it back."}

reteach:s3c:other.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's test your answer the way mathematicians do: put it back in and see if the sides still match."}
{"t":"math","id":"r3a","latex":"2x + 4 = 10","expr":"2*x + 4 = 10"}
{"t":"math","id":"r3b","latex":"\\text{try } x = 5: \\; 2(5) + 4 = 14","expr":"2*5 + 4 = 14"}
{"t":"say","text":"That gave 14, not 10, so a guess of 5 breaks the balance and cannot be the solution."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"r3b","note":"left side ≠ 10"}
{"t":"say","text":"Instead, undo the plus 4 first, on both sides, and keep every term moving together."}
{"t":"step","id":"r3c","latex":"2x = 6","expr":"2*x = 6","why":"subtract 4 from both sides","from":"r3a"}
{"t":"step","id":"r3d","latex":"x = 3","expr":"x = 3","why":"divide the whole of both sides by 2","from":"r3c"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now the same test passes: putting this answer back gives exactly 10."}
{"t":"math","id":"r3e","latex":"2(3) + 4 = 10","expr":"2*3 + 4 = 10"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"r3e","note":"both sides match"}
{"t":"say","text":"Try your checkpoint again, and put your answer back into 4x + 3 to see if it gives 27."}

reteach:s4c:other.text:
{"t":"say","text":"I think you may have picked a point because it showed a 5 or a 20 from the equation."}
{"t":"write","id":"r4a","text":"A solution is an input x that makes 3x + 5 come out as 20.","role":"line"}
{"t":"say","text":"So let's test inputs in a table and watch what comes out."}
{"t":"table","id":"r4t","head":["x (input)","3x + 5 (output)"],"rows":[["0","5"],["1","8"],["2","11"],["3","14"]],"fn":"3*x + 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"Take x equals 0: the output is 5, not 20, so 0 fails even though 5 appears."}
{"t":"math","id":"r4m","latex":"3(0) + 5 = 5 \\neq 20","expr":"3*0 + 5 = 5"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"underline","target":"r4m","note":"output must be 20"}
{"t":"say","text":"Each step of 1 in x adds 3 to the output, so keep going until it lands on 20, then try again."}

solution:s1c.text:
{"t":"page","title":"Worked solution"}
{"t":"say","text":"Let's work it through together, keeping the scale level at every step."}
{"t":"math","id":"sol1a","latex":"3x + 5 = 20","expr":"3*x + 5 = 20"}
{"t":"say","text":"Subtract 5 from both sides, so the left loses 5 and the right loses 5 as well."}
{"t":"step","id":"sol1b","latex":"3x + 5 - 5 = 20 - 5","expr":"3*x + 5 - 5 = 20 - 5","why":"subtract 5 from both sides","from":"sol1a"}
{"t":"step","id":"sol1c","latex":"3x = 15","expr":"3*x = 15","why":"simplify both sides","from":"sol1b"}
{"t":"say","text":"Your slip was changing only one side, or giving each side a different move, which tips the scale."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"sol1b","note":"same move on both sides"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now 3x means 3 times x, so divide both sides by 3 to undo it."}
{"t":"step","id":"sol1d","latex":"\\frac{3x}{3} = \\frac{15}{3}","expr":"(3*x)/3 = 15/3","why":"divide both sides by 3","from":"sol1c"}
{"t":"say","text":"Both sides simplify, and x is left alone on the left."}
{"t":"solutions","id":"sol1s","of":"sol1d","var":"x","values":[5],"latex":"x = 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"This same-move-on-both-sides idea will come back in practice, so you'll get another go."}

solution:s2c.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's work it through together, starting with the equation 2x plus 7 equals 19."}
{"t":"page","title":"Worked solution: 2x + 7 = 19"}
{"t":"math","id":"sol2e","latex":"2x + 7 = 19","expr":"2*x + 7 = 19"}
{"t":"say","text":"To undo plus 7 we subtract 7, and the balance rule says we do it to both sides."}
{"t":"step","id":"sol2s","latex":"2x + 7 - 7 = 19 - 7","expr":"2*x + 7 - 7 = 19 - 7","why":"subtract 7 from both sides","from":"sol2e"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"underline","target":"sol2s","note":"−7 on both sides"}
{"t":"say","text":"You added 7 once, and once left the right side alone, which tips the balance; both sides must change."}
{"t":"say","text":"On the left, plus 7 minus 7 cancels, and on the right, 19 minus 7 is 12."}
{"t":"step","id":"sol2r","latex":"2x = 12","expr":"2*x = 12","why":"7 − 7 cancels; 19 − 7 = 12","from":"sol2s"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"sol2r","note":"right side is 12"}
{"t":"say","text":"So the right side is 12, and the equation is now 2x equals 12."}
{"t":"say","text":"This subtract-from-both-sides move will come back in practice, so you'll get another go."}

solution:s3c.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's work it through together, starting with the equation exactly as given."}
{"t":"math","id":"sol3a","latex":"4x + 3 = 27","expr":"4*x + 3 = 27"}
{"t":"say","text":"First undo the plus 3 by subtracting 3 from both sides, so the balance holds."}
{"t":"step","id":"sol3b","latex":"4x = 24","expr":"4*x = 24","why":"subtract 3 from both sides","from":"sol3a"}
{"t":"say","text":"Here is the slip: 24 is the value of 4x, not of x, so we are not finished."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"sol3b","note":"this is 4x, not x"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now undo the times 4 by dividing both sides by 4."}
{"t":"step","id":"sol3c","latex":"x = \\frac{24}{4}","expr":"x = 24/4","why":"divide both sides by 4","from":"sol3b"}
{"t":"solutions","id":"sol3d","of":"sol3c","var":"x","values":[6],"latex":"x = 6"}
{"t":"say","text":"The other slip was adding 3 to get 30; the plus 3 must be subtracted, which gives 24."}
{"t":"math","id":"sol3e","latex":"4(6) + 3 = 27","expr":"4*6 + 3 = 27"}
{"t":"say","text":"Putting 6 back in gives 27, so it works, and this two-step idea will come back in practice."}

solution:s4c.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's solve it step by step, then see where the answer sits on the graph."}
{"t":"math","id":"sol4a","latex":"3x + 5 = 20","expr":"3*x + 5 = 20"}
{"t":"say","text":"First undo the plus 5 by subtracting 5 from both sides."}
{"t":"step","id":"sol4b","latex":"3x = 15","expr":"3*x = 15","why":"subtract 5 from both sides","from":"sol4a"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now undo the times 3 by dividing both sides by 3."}
{"t":"step","id":"sol4c","latex":"x = 5","expr":"x = 5","why":"divide both sides by 3","from":"sol4b"}
{"t":"say","text":"So the solution is x equals 5, and that is the answer, not the height 20."}
{"t":"solutions","id":"sol4s","of":"sol4a","var":"x","values":[5],"latex":"x = 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"You may have tapped a point where the line meets a different place; the solution is where the two graphs meet."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s4pb","note":"x = 5: the line reaches height 20 here"}
{"t":"say","text":"Its x-coordinate, 5, is the solution; we will use this idea again in practice."}

lost:1:1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"No problem, let's start with something smaller: a plain sum that is already true."}
{"t":"math","id":"l1k1a","latex":"3 + 2 = 5","expr":"3 + 2 = 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"Both sides are the same amount, five, so the equals sign says they balance."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"underline","target":"l1k1a","note":"both sides are 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now add 4 to the left side only, and see what happens."}
{"t":"math","id":"l1k1b","latex":"3 + 2 + 4 = 9 \\neq 5","expr":"3 + 2 + 4 = 9"}
{"t":"say","text":"The left is now 9 and the right is still 5, so the balance is broken."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"l1k1b","note":"9 is not 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"Add 4 to both sides instead, and both come out as nine."}
{"t":"math","id":"l1k1c","latex":"3 + 2 + 4 = 5 + 4","expr":"3 + 2 + 4 = 5 + 4"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"l1k1c","note":"same move both sides, still equal"}
{"t":"say","text":"That is the whole idea, so let's go back to the question and try it on 3x + 5 = 20."}

lost:2:1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Let's slow down and start with a number puzzle you can do in your head."}
{"t":"write","id":"l2k1a","text":"I think of a number, add 5, and get 8.","role":"line"}
{"t":"math","id":"l2k1b","latex":"? + 5 = 8","expr":"3 + 5 = 8"}
{"t":"say","text":"To find the mystery number, undo the adding: take 5 away from the 8."}
{"t":"math","id":"l2k1c","latex":"8 - 5 = 3","expr":"8 - 5 = 3"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"l2k1c","note":"undo +5 with −5"}
{"t":"say","text":"Now a slightly bigger one: a mystery lump, plus 5, makes 20."}
{"t":"math","id":"l2k1d","latex":"\\text{lump} + 5 = 20","expr":"15 + 5 = 20"}
{"t":"say","text":"Take 5 away from the 20 and you find the lump is 15."}
{"t":"math","id":"l2k1e","latex":"20 - 5 = 15","expr":"20 - 5 = 15"}
{"t":"say","text":"In our equation, the lump is 3x, so taking 5 from both sides leaves 3x equal to 15."}
{"t":"math","id":"l2k1f","latex":"3x + 5 = 20 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 3x = 20 - 5 = 15","expr":"20 - 5 = 15"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"l2k1f","note":"same move as the lump"}
{"t":"say","text":"That is all step two asks for, so let's go back to the lesson and try the question."}

lost:3:1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"No worries, let's start with something smaller: three bags, each holding the same number of sweets."}
{"t":"math","id":"l3k1a","latex":"3 \\times 4 = 12","expr":"3*4 = 12"}
{"t":"say","text":"If I tell you three bags hold 12 sweets in total, you would share 12 into 3 equal groups."}
{"t":"math","id":"l3k1b","latex":"12 \\div 3 = 4","expr":"12/3 = 4"}
{"t":"say","text":"Dividing is the move that undoes multiplying, so now we use it on three x equals 15."}
{"t":"math","id":"l3k1c","latex":"3x = 15","expr":"3*x = 15"}
{"t":"step","id":"l3k1d","latex":"\\frac{3x}{3} = \\frac{15}{3}","expr":"(3*x)/3 = 15/3","why":"divide both sides by 3","from":"l3k1c"}
{"t":"say","text":"On the left the 3 cancels, leaving one x, and on the right 15 shared into 3 is 5."}
{"t":"step","id":"l3k1e","latex":"x = 5","expr":"x = 5","why":"simplify both sides","from":"l3k1d"}
{"t":"say","text":"Same idea as the bags: each one holds 5. Now have another go at the question."}

lost:4:1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"No problem, let's forget the graph and just try some numbers in 3x + 5."}
{"t":"table","id":"l4k1t","head":["x","3x + 5"],"rows":[["1","8"],["3","14"],["5","20"]],"fn":"3*x + 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"Each row is one guess for x, and the right column is what the rule gives back."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"l4k1t","note":"x = 5 gives 20"}
{"t":"say","text":"We wanted 20, and only x equals 5 gives it, so that is the solution."}
{"t":"math","id":"l4k1m","latex":"3(5) + 5 = 20","expr":"3*5 + 5 = 20"}
{"t":"say","text":"On the graph, that same row is the point where the slanted line reaches height 20."}
{"t":"point","id":"l4k1p","plot":"s4p","at":[5,20],"label":"x = 5, height 20","on":"s4f1"}
{"t":"say","text":"Take another look at the graph, and try the tap question again."}

answer:s1q1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Good question: an equation is a claim that both sides are equal, and we want to keep that claim true."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"underline","target":"s1e","note":"this claim is true right now"}
{"t":"say","text":"If we take 5 off only the left, the sides stop being equal, so the claim breaks."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s1x","note":"left changed, right didn't"}
{"t":"say","text":"Take 5 off both sides and the scale stays level, so the claim is still true."}
{"t":"math","id":"a1m","latex":"3x + 5 - 5 = 20 - 5","expr":"3*x + 5 - 5 = 20 - 5"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"s1ok","note":"same move, still equal"}
{"t":"say","text":"So doing the same to both sides means any x that worked before still works after."}
{"t":"say","text":"What do you think would happen to the balance if we took 5 off the left and only 2 off the right?"}

answer:s1q2.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Yes, the same balance rule works for multiplying and dividing: do the same thing to both pans."}
{"t":"math","id":"a2m","latex":"3x = 20 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\frac{3x}{3} = \\frac{20}{3}","expr":"(3*x)/3 = 20/3"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"a2m","note":"divide both sides by 3"}
{"t":"say","text":"Dividing the left side by 3 but not the right would tip the scale, just like subtracting from one side."}
{"t":"write","id":"a2w","text":"Left ÷ 3, right untouched: scale tips","role":"note"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s1x","note":"one side changed = unbalanced"}
{"t":"say","text":"Only one thing is special: never multiply or divide both sides by zero, because that wrecks the balance."}
{"t":"say","text":"So what would you do to both sides of 4x = 12 to leave x alone?"}

answer:s2q1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Look at how 3x + 5 was built: x was multiplied by 3 first, then 5 was added."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s2e","note":"×3 first, then +5"}
{"t":"say","text":"To undo it, go backwards: undo the last thing done first, which was the plus 5."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"arrow","target":"s2s","note":"undo +5 first"}
{"t":"say","text":"The opposite of adding 5 is subtracting 5, and we do it to both sides to keep the balance."}
{"t":"step","id":"a3s","latex":"3x + 5 - 5 = 20 - 5","expr":"3*x + 5 - 5 = 20 - 5","why":"opposite of +5, on both sides","from":"s2e"}
{"t":"say","text":"That leaves 3x, which was made by multiplying, so the next undo is the opposite: divide by 3."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"s2r","note":"×3 is next to undo"}
{"t":"say","text":"Here's a check: if x is multiplied by 4 and then 2 is subtracted, what do you undo first?"}

answer:s2q2.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Good question: the order of the sides doesn't matter, as long as both sides get the same move."}
{"t":"math","id":"a4r","latex":"20 - 5 = 15","expr":"20 - 5 = 15"}
{"t":"math","id":"a4l","latex":"3x + 5 - 5 = 3x","expr":"3*x + 5 - 5 = 3*x"}
{"t":"say","text":"Right side first or left side first, you still land on the same line, 3x equals 15."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"s2r","note":"same result either way"}
{"t":"say","text":"What breaks the balance is changing only one side, like this wrong line here."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s2w2","note":"only one side changed"}
{"t":"say","text":"So if you subtract 5 from the right side first, what must you then do to the left?"}

answer:s3q1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Look at 3x = 15 as a balance: the left side equals the right side."}
{"t":"math","id":"a5a","latex":"3x = 15","expr":"3*x = 15"}
{"t":"say","text":"If I divide only the left by 3, that side shrinks to x, but the right stays 15."}
{"t":"math","id":"a5b","latex":"x \\neq 15","expr":"3*x/3 = x"}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s3bad","note":"only one side changed, so the balance broke"}
{"t":"say","text":"Divide both sides by 3, and the balance holds, because both sides shrink to a third."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"s3s","note":"same move on both sides"}
{"t":"say","text":"So if you divide the left by 3, what must you do to the right?"}

answer:s3q2.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Great question: to check an answer, put it back into the original equation and see if both sides match."}
{"t":"math","id":"a6o","latex":"3x + 5 = 20","expr":"3*x + 5 = 20"}
{"t":"say","text":"Our solution is x equals 5, so swap every x for 5."}
{"t":"math","id":"a6l","latex":"3(5) + 5 = 15 + 5 = 20","expr":"3*5 + 5 = 15 + 5 = 20"}
{"t":"say","text":"The left side comes to 20, which is exactly the right side, so x equals 5 works."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"box","target":"a6l","note":"both sides match"}
{"t":"say","text":"If you had got something other than 20, you would know a step went wrong and could hunt for it."}
{"t":"say","text":"Try it yourself: if someone claimed x equals 4, what would the left side come to?"}

answer:s4q1.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Think of the two sloped and flat lines as two machines that each give a height for every x."}
{"t":"math","id":"a7m1","latex":"y = 3x + 5 \\quad\\text{and}\\quad y = 20","expr":"y = 3*x + 5"}
{"t":"say","text":"Where the lines cross, both machines give the same height at the same x."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s4pb","note":"same x, same height"}
{"t":"say","text":"Same height means 3x + 5 and 20 are equal, and that is exactly our equation."}
{"t":"math","id":"a7m2","latex":"3x + 5 = 20","expr":"3*x + 5 = 20"}
{"t":"say","text":"Anywhere else, like x = 0, the heights differ: 5 on one line, 20 on the other."}
{"t":"line","id":"a7l","plot":"s4p","from":[0,5],"to":[0,20],"label":"gap of 15","dashed":true}
{"t":"say","text":"So at x = 0 the sides are not equal, but at x = 5 the gap closes. Does that make sense?"}

answer:s4q2.text:
{"t":"say","text":"Yes, two lines can miss each other, when they have exactly the same steepness and never meet."}
{"t":"write","id":"a8n","text":"Same steepness, different height: parallel, no crossing","role":"note"}
{"t":"say","text":"For example, 3x + 5 = 3x + 20 would give two parallel lines, so it has no solution."}
{"t":"write","id":"a8e","text":"3x + 5 = 3x + 20 → no solution","role":"line"}
{"t":"say","text":"Ours is different: our line climbs, while y = 20 stays flat, so they must meet."}
{"t":"mark","kind":"circle","target":"s4pb","note":"they cross here"}
{"t":"say","text":"So if one line climbs and the other is flat, could they ever stay apart?"}

recap.text:
{"t":"idea","id":"i1","text":"An equation is a balance: whatever you do to one side, you do to the other.","seg":1}
{"t":"idea","id":"i2","text":"To undo plus or minus, do the opposite to both sides, which leaves the x term alone.","seg":2}
{"t":"idea","id":"i3","text":"To undo times, divide both sides by the same number, and x is left on its own.","seg":3}
{"t":"idea","id":"i4","text":"The solution is the x where the graph of the left side meets the line of the right side.","seg":4}
{"t":"say","text":"You first guessed subtracting 5 from both sides, so what do you think now: why does it work?"}
{"t":"check","id":"rp1","kind":"choice","idea":"i1","prompt":"You subtract 6 from the left side of x + 6 = 15. What must you do to keep the equation true?","options":[{"id":"a","text":"Subtract 6 from the right side too","correct":true},{"id":"b","text":"Add 6 to the right side","correct":false,"misconception":"Did the opposite operation on the right side instead of matching the left"},{"id":"c","text":"Leave the right side alone","correct":false,"misconception":"Thought only one side needs to change because the other already shows a number"}],"explain":"Both sides must change the same way, or the balance tips.","hint":"Picture a balance: what happens if you only take weight off one pan?"}
{"t":"check","id":"rp2","kind":"number","idea":"i2","prompt":"Solve 4x − 7 = 21. What is x?","answer":"7","anticipated":[{"value":"3.5","misconception":"Subtracted 7 from 21 instead of adding it, then divided by 4"},{"value":"28","misconception":"Stopped at 4x = 28 and forgot to divide by 4"}],"explain":"Add 7 to get 4x = 28, then divide by 4 to get x = 7.","hint":"Undo the minus 7 first, then undo the times 4."}
{"t":"check","id":"rp3","kind":"number","idea":"i4","prompt":"The line y = 2x + 1 meets the line y = 9. At what x do they meet?","answer":"4","anticipated":[{"value":"4.5","misconception":"Divided 9 by 2 and forgot the plus 1"},{"value":"8","misconception":"Subtracted 1 but stopped at 2x = 8 without dividing"}],"explain":"They meet where 2x + 1 = 9, so 2x = 8 and x = 4.","hint":"At the meeting point, the height of the first line equals 9."}
